WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:07.652
Module three covers static inputs: the geometry and structure of the network that a twin needs before any live data can make sense.

2
00:00:08.552 --> 00:00:11.154
Reality capture comes in two families.

3
00:00:11.154 --> 00:00:17.590
Laser scanning, from a tripod, a vehicle or an aircraft, measures millions of points directly.

4
00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:21.903
Photogrammetry rebuilds 3D shape from overlapping drone photos.

5
00:00:21.903 --> 00:00:25.190
Accuracy, cost and coverage decide which to use.

6
00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:28.325
Raw point clouds are only the start.

7
00:00:28.325 --> 00:00:40.372
They are registered into one coordinate system, classified into road, kerb, vegetation and structures, and then features such as lane edges and signs are extracted into a network or a BIM model.

8
00:00:41.272 --> 00:00:43.668
A traffic model needs more than geometry.

9
00:00:43.668 --> 00:00:52.610
It needs nodes, links, lanes and the turns allowed between them, the signal programmes, and the demand: how many trips travel between each pair of zones.

10
00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:56.026
Open standards keep this data usable.

11
00:00:56.026 --> 00:01:01.057
IFC 4.3 extends building information modelling to roads, rail and bridges.

12
00:01:01.057 --> 00:01:05.205
CityGML describes city objects at different levels of detail.

13
00:01:05.205 --> 00:01:09.284
OpenDRIVE describes lane-level roads for driving simulators.

14
00:01:10.184 --> 00:01:13.276
Capture is expensive, so plan it to serve many uses.

15
00:01:13.276 --> 00:01:19.282
Agree the coordinate system, the accuracy you need and the output formats before anyone goes on site.
